  "abstract": "We give a natural-language derivation of the ordinary Riemann hypothesis from the arithmetic and period comparison theorems in the Lean proof released by Levent Alpöge with Claude. We identify the closed proofs of these two inputs before the zero-free theorem is invoked. The arithmetic theorem bounds strict Selmer lengths on a power interval for each eligible pair of primes. The geometric theorem selects a single family of periods on which the integral indices, signed heights and archimedean estimates hold together. We show where the common finite divisor cancels in the signed height calculation and how the ranks of high-slope images bound the remaining row slope. Translation on the real dyadic Galois group leaves four character sums. Bounds on the three auxiliary characters then control the centered von Mangoldt sum throughout arbitrarily late power intervals. For any proposed zero to the right of the critical line, a localized Mellin derivative isolates its pole and contradicts this bound. We also give an alternative argument within the rank-four cohomological count. Under the prescribed modularity and seed hypotheses on each required chart and coefficient extension, one projected Hecke ideal suffices for its potential modularity tower. For a matched cuspidal attachment, polarized solvable descent and a count with prescribed finite unit characters bound the rank-four contribution under the original integral comparison. The deep mixed-character Selmer lengths and the compatible signed-height and period estimates enter through the published intermediate theorems.",
